1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This is a hybrid ensemble deep learning system that identifies blood cancer
subtypes from microscope images of blood smears.

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:14,099
Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia is the most common childhood cancer. Diagnosis
starts with a blood smear under a microscope, which is slow, and the three
malignant stages look very similar. Our goal is to classify an image into
Benign, early Pre-B, Pre-B or Pro-B automatically, and to show why.

3
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:22,500
Every image is sent to three separately fine-tuned convolutional networks:
MobileNetV2, EfficientNetB0 and DenseNet121. Each outputs four probabilities.
The twelve numbers are combined in two ways, weighted soft voting and a stacked
logistic-regression meta-classifier. That pair is the hybrid ensemble.

4
00:00:22,199 --> 00:00:29,899
On the Preview page the user chooses a blood smear image and presses Submit.
Within about two seconds the ensemble returns the predicted class and a
confidence. This image is Benign at 97 percent.

5
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:36,299
The probability bars show how the confidence is shared across all four classes,
so a close call is visible at a glance. This image is an early Pre-B malignant
cell.

6
00:00:35,999 --> 00:00:42,699
Underneath, each backbone's own vote and confidence are listed. If the three
networks disagree, the app flags the result for manual review.

7
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:50,099
The Grad-CAM heatmap explains the decision. Red marks the regions that most
increased the predicted class score: the large nucleated blast cells, not the
background.

8
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:56,499
Across the test set, correct predictions show focused heat on the cell; the few
misclassified cases show diffuse heat, which is itself a useful warning sign.

9
00:00:56,199 --> 00:01:04,899
On 487 unseen test images, MobileNetV2 reached 96.92 %, EfficientNetB0 91.38 %
and DenseNet121 98.97 %. The stacked ensemble reached 98.97 % accuracy with a
macro F1 of 0.988.

10
00:01:04,599 --> 00:01:11,299
The confusion matrix shows only five errors in 487 images. Every class is
recalled above 98 percent, and no benign image was ever called malignant.

11
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,700
The ROC curves confirm it: an area under the curve of 1.000, meaning the
ensemble separates all four classes almost perfectly.

12
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:25,100
In summary: a stacked hybrid ensemble reaching 98.97 % accuracy on held-out
data, with explanations on every prediction, delivered as a complete web
application. This is a research project and is not for clinical use. Thank you.
